<body>
  <main class="lesson-shell" aria-label="Lesson 1: Anatomy">
    <article class="lesson-card">
      <div class="lesson-inner">
        <header class="lesson-header">
          <p class="eyebrow">Facial Volume Harmony</p>
          <h1>Lesson 1: Anatomy</h1>
          <div class="intro-rule" aria-hidden="true"></div>
        </header>

        <section class="lesson-content">
          <p class="lead">With age, the face may gradually appear longer and lose its sense of width and volume. Facial features can become less full, while the areas around the eyes and mouth may appear more recessed.</p>

          <p>Over time, facial contours may lose definition as soft tissues and muscles gradually decrease in tone and elasticity, reducing their supportive function.</p>

          <p>As a result, these changes often become more noticeable in the lower face — the jawline may appear less defined, a sense of fullness or heaviness under the chin may develop, and the visual clarity of the so-called “youth angle” may diminish.</p>

          <div class="soft-callout">
            <p>This naturally raises the question: what contributes to a harmonious, symmetrical, and well-supported face?</p>
          </div>

          <p>One of the key factors is the condition and functional activity of the facial muscles, as well as the position of the tongue within the oral cavity. Tongue posture, breathing patterns, and swallowing habits play an important role in shaping facial proportions and overall balance.</p>

          <p>A gentle and consistent contact of the tongue with the palate, combined with nasal breathing and physiologically appropriate swallowing, helps support balance in the facial area and contributes to a more open, harmonious facial expression.</p>

          <p>It is believed that the foundations of these functional skills are established early in life, in part through natural feeding processes that require complex and coordinated work of the entire oral system. During this time, fundamental patterns of tongue posture and swallowing are formed.</p>

          <p>Within this course, we will explore principles of conscious tongue posture awareness. However, the program is not limited to this approach alone.</p>

          <figure class="image-wrap">
            <img src="data:image/png;base64," alt="Anatomical illustration of facial muscles." loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
          </figure>

          <p class="closing">You will also be introduced to gentle manual techniques for working with the soft tissues of the face. This integrated approach — combining functional awareness with soft-tissue work — helps develop a deeper sense of support, balance, and visual expressiveness of the face.</p>
        </section>
      </div>
